WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.368
-You guys, if you watch
this show at all,

00:00:02.368 --> 00:00:04.337
you know that I love
"Better Call Saul."

00:00:04.337 --> 00:00:05.638
And if you watch
"Better Call Saul,"

00:00:05.638 --> 00:00:07.140
You know that
Rhea Seehorn's portrayal

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:09.775
of Kim Wexler deserves
100 Emmys,

00:00:09.775 --> 00:00:12.078
and I'm very happy to say
please welcome back to the show,

00:00:12.078 --> 00:00:15.915
Emmy nominated ratio
Rhea Seehorn, everybody.

00:00:15.915 --> 00:00:18.117
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:18.117 --> 00:00:24.323 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:24.323 --> 00:00:30.597 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:30.597 --> 00:00:32.164
Welcome back to the show, Rhea.
Thank you.

00:00:32.164 --> 00:00:33.866
-I'm so happy to have you.

00:00:33.866 --> 00:00:35.201
-Emmy-nominated
Seth Meyers Show.

00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:38.138
-Thank you, we both had
a very nice day last week.

00:00:38.138 --> 00:00:39.506
We both got very nice news.

00:00:39.506 --> 00:00:42.308
I was so genuinely happy to hear

00:00:42.308 --> 00:00:44.177
that you got a long overdue
Emmy nomination.

00:00:44.177 --> 00:00:45.477
Where were you --
-Did you guys see that tweet

00:00:45.477 --> 00:00:47.446
he sent that morning?

00:00:47.446 --> 00:00:49.782
Seth, you said that you were
more happy mine,

00:00:49.782 --> 00:00:51.183
almost more happy
for mine nomination.

00:00:51.183 --> 00:00:52.919
-Yes, I didn't lie.

00:00:52.919 --> 00:00:54.820
I said I was almost
as happy, yeah.

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:55.988
It would be weird
if you said --

00:00:55.988 --> 00:00:57.390
-Yeah, I think that I'm

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:59.258
just not a good enough
actor to pull it off.

00:00:59.258 --> 00:01:01.127
Like, awards mean
nothing to me.

00:01:01.127 --> 00:01:04.330
-I know.
-But my friend, Rhea.

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:06.566
But this is very exciting.
You were --

00:01:06.566 --> 00:01:09.235
I read -- I read an article
about that you were overseas.

00:01:09.235 --> 00:01:10.670
You were on vacation
when you got the news.

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:12.305
-Yes, I was in London
for the first time.

00:01:12.305 --> 00:01:14.674
It was amazing, with my fiancee.

00:01:14.674 --> 00:01:18.511
-I feel like the last three
years or so there's been like

00:01:18.511 --> 00:01:20.380
talk of like
wouldn't it be great if.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:22.848
So you probably had
some expectation the last

00:01:22.848 --> 00:01:24.216
three years that you've had
to deal with some like

00:01:24.216 --> 00:01:25.384
disappointment, right?

00:01:25.384 --> 00:01:26.618
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:01:26.618 --> 00:01:28.354
-I mean, like, it's hard.

00:01:28.354 --> 00:01:31.024
I mean, I think you're not
supposed to care,

00:01:31.024 --> 00:01:32.725
but at the same time
you're sort of an ass

00:01:32.725 --> 00:01:34.693
if you don't care at all.
-Yeah.

00:01:34.693 --> 00:01:37.029
-Somebody saying nice
things like that, so...

00:01:37.029 --> 00:01:40.833
And then of course you
and critics and amazing fans

00:01:40.833 --> 00:01:43.302
have been lovely to me and
saying that they wanted me

00:01:43.302 --> 00:01:45.738
to be nominated, so yeah,
I would sit there holding

00:01:45.738 --> 00:01:48.807
my fiancee's hand and the
announcements would come out.

00:01:48.807 --> 00:01:51.177
We'll be like, "Alright,
let's make oatmeal."

00:01:51.177 --> 00:01:54.747
Moving on, and this year
we were -- I guess it was around

00:01:54.747 --> 00:01:59.151
4:30 London time so I fully
opted for day drinking.

00:01:59.151 --> 00:02:00.586
-Okay, got you.
Very smart.

00:02:00.586 --> 00:02:02.154
You know I approve.
-Right.

00:02:02.154 --> 00:02:06.960
I was at the -- it's called the
American Bar at the Savoy Hotel.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:08.594
-That's really
cool that you went to London

00:02:08.594 --> 00:02:09.695
and went to the American bar.

00:02:09.695 --> 00:02:11.831
-I know.
Okay, by the way --

00:02:11.831 --> 00:02:14.133
-Did you go to McDonald's
while you were there?

00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:15.968
You've got to go
to the McDonald's.

00:02:15.968 --> 00:02:17.737
-No, but... [ Laughs ]

00:02:17.737 --> 00:02:19.239
There are patriotic things.

00:02:19.239 --> 00:02:21.140
I'm not exactly sure
why they call it that.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:23.308
It just like -- it was in my
little lonely planet guide.

00:02:23.308 --> 00:02:24.443
-Okay, got you.

00:02:24.443 --> 00:02:26.812
-Anyway, sorry.
Yeah, I like the books.

00:02:26.812 --> 00:02:30.249
But we went and Jennifer Bryan,
our costume designer

00:02:30.249 --> 00:02:31.451
from "Better Call Saul"
was there.

00:02:31.451 --> 00:02:33.719
And I was like, "I'm putting
my phone away,

00:02:33.719 --> 00:02:35.654
I'm just going to forget it."

00:02:35.654 --> 00:02:38.424
And then my fiancee
squeezed my hand.

00:02:38.424 --> 00:02:40.226
He was like I have
business deal,

00:02:40.226 --> 00:02:41.727
and he's looking at something,
and then he said

00:02:41.727 --> 00:02:44.130
"Babe, you just got it.
-That's so exciting.

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:45.632
-And then the whole bar knew,
and I didn't know

00:02:45.632 --> 00:02:47.433
they knew who I was, and then
they brought champagne over

00:02:47.433 --> 00:02:48.601
and clapped for me.

00:02:48.601 --> 00:02:50.803
-Oh, see, if you were at
the British bar,

00:02:50.803 --> 00:02:51.938
they would have had no idea.

00:02:51.938 --> 00:02:53.673
[ Laughter ]

00:02:53.673 --> 00:02:54.841
You were smart.
You played it right.

00:02:54.841 --> 00:02:56.609
-They actually watch "Saul"
a lot over there.

00:02:56.609 --> 00:02:57.943
-I bet.
-People watch me a lot.

00:02:57.943 --> 00:03:00.713
-I mean, look, I think "Saul"
crosses all cultures.

00:03:00.713 --> 00:03:02.281
It's a perfect show.
That's -- there's no reason --

00:03:02.281 --> 00:03:05.552
-Thank you.
-I would imagine that the --

00:03:05.552 --> 00:03:09.321
the rest of the day is then just
sort of a perfect day, right?

00:03:09.321 --> 00:03:13.325
Because for me it -- it's that
thing of, like,

00:03:13.325 --> 00:03:14.794
I don't know if you have that,
like a wave of just

00:03:14.794 --> 00:03:17.029
relief of like, "Oh, I don't
have to, like,

00:03:17.029 --> 00:03:18.531
think about this anymore."
-Yes.

00:03:18.531 --> 00:03:19.865
-Like, whether or not
this will happen.

00:03:19.865 --> 00:03:21.133
-But also I don't have to spend
the day pretending

00:03:21.133 --> 00:03:22.635
that it doesn't matter.

00:03:22.635 --> 00:03:24.504
Isn't that what you normally
have to do?

00:03:24.504 --> 00:03:26.238
'Cause all day long people
are like, you have to answer

00:03:26.238 --> 00:03:29.142
people's texts saying like,
"Oh, my God, this is awful.

00:03:29.142 --> 00:03:30.709
You were snubbed."

00:03:30.709 --> 00:03:32.011
It's just like, you know.
-Yeah.

00:03:32.011 --> 00:03:33.179
I'd like -- right.
-"I'm totally fine."

00:03:33.179 --> 00:03:35.181
-In another world I'm saying
it my wife, like,

00:03:35.181 --> 00:03:36.715
"Look, it doesn't matter, we
have three healthy kids,"

00:03:36.715 --> 00:03:38.517
where I barely care about that.

00:03:38.517 --> 00:03:39.719
-Right.

00:03:39.719 --> 00:03:41.921
[ Laughter ]

00:03:41.921 --> 00:03:43.990
Right. Today you get to be
honest about your priorities.

00:03:43.990 --> 00:03:45.124
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:03:45.124 --> 00:03:47.926
I'm like, "Look, I'm glad
they're healthy!"

00:03:47.926 --> 00:03:51.230
-"But I got an --"
-"But I am an Emmy nominee."

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:53.199
-Exactly.
-I actually -- it was very sweet

00:03:53.199 --> 00:03:56.469
because I -- I had gone out
for like -- I had gone

00:03:56.469 --> 00:03:59.038
for a run, and I came home
and my wife had made --

00:03:59.038 --> 00:04:01.607
the oldest son was holding a
sign saying "congratulations."

00:04:01.607 --> 00:04:03.276
-Aw.
-Which was really sweet,

00:04:03.276 --> 00:04:06.512
and then, they -- my kids were
like excited, and then he --

00:04:06.512 --> 00:04:09.114
the six-year-old turned to give
me a hug, and just whacked

00:04:09.114 --> 00:04:11.550
the four-year-old the in back
of the head with the sign.

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:14.654
And it was -- it was that --
it really was a very sweet,

00:04:14.654 --> 00:04:16.255
grounding moment,
because in that moment

00:04:16.255 --> 00:04:18.557
I was like, "Oh, I'm actually --
I feel worse that my

00:04:18.557 --> 00:04:20.792
four-year-old got, like,
T-boned by this sign..."

00:04:20.792 --> 00:04:22.160
-Right.
-"...than I feel good

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:23.396
that I got a nomination."

00:04:23.396 --> 00:04:24.830
-Yeah, you have to switch
priorities, I like it.

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:26.498
-Um, and then --
-But also you were day drinking.

00:04:26.498 --> 00:04:27.834
-Yeah, I was day drinking, too.

00:04:27.834 --> 00:04:29.369
Even though it was --
the time difference was,

00:04:29.369 --> 00:04:30.836
it was way too early.
-Totally fine.

00:04:30.836 --> 00:04:33.105
Totally fine.

00:04:33.105 --> 00:04:35.174
-You -- I want to stress,
if anybody's watching this

00:04:35.174 --> 00:04:36.742
and worried about spoilers.

00:04:36.742 --> 00:04:38.177
I don't want anything
spoiled for me,

00:04:38.177 --> 00:04:40.079
I don't want to spoil the show
for anyone else.

00:04:40.079 --> 00:04:43.282
And we are in a weird time right
now where we're having

00:04:43.282 --> 00:04:45.384
an interview before tonight's
episode airs,

00:04:45.384 --> 00:04:48.054
but when this airs,
that will have happened.

00:04:48.054 --> 00:04:49.722
-Okay.
-So this is a weird, like,

00:04:49.722 --> 00:04:51.324
we're in like a temporal,
like, doughnut hole.

00:04:51.324 --> 00:04:52.758
-Oh.
-Yeah, so, it's a cool --

00:04:52.758 --> 00:04:54.827
but -- so don't give
anything away.

00:04:54.827 --> 00:04:56.662
But I know, because
it's been announced

00:04:56.662 --> 00:04:59.732
that Carol Burnett...
-Yes.

00:04:59.732 --> 00:05:02.034
-...is going to be in one
of the upcoming episodes.

00:05:02.034 --> 00:05:04.002
-Have you met her?
-I have met her one time.

00:05:04.002 --> 00:05:05.271
And it is like --
-She's amazing.

00:05:05.271 --> 00:05:07.974
-It's amazing.
Super sharp, super funny.

00:05:07.974 --> 00:05:09.908
-Yup.
-And the most important thing,

00:05:09.908 --> 00:05:11.377
and I don't know if you feel
this way about when you

00:05:11.377 --> 00:05:13.245
meet people you look up to,
super nice.

00:05:13.245 --> 00:05:14.480
-Incredibly nice, yeah.

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:17.350
She's one of those people
where -- I mean, to me,

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:20.152
obviously she's a legend to lots
of people, she's a hero to me,

00:05:20.152 --> 00:05:23.456
and just an icon and someone
I have admired forever.

00:05:23.456 --> 00:05:25.223
And so you never know what it's
going to be like

00:05:25.223 --> 00:05:27.927
when you meet those people.
She could not be nicer.

00:05:27.927 --> 00:05:31.130
She could not be more genuine,
and generous

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:33.332
and she's a huge fan
of the show.

00:05:33.332 --> 00:05:35.735
And so when they said
"Carol wants to know

00:05:35.735 --> 00:05:39.806
if you want to go to dinner,"
I was so excited

00:05:39.806 --> 00:05:41.974
that I -- and I went,
and I was shaking

00:05:41.974 --> 00:05:43.376
and she started asking me about

00:05:43.376 --> 00:05:45.177
Kim Wexler's scenes,
like, in detail.

00:05:45.177 --> 00:05:48.548
She's a huge Kim Wexler fan
and then I burst into tears.

00:05:48.548 --> 00:05:51.084
Like, she's just -- I mean,
it's Carol Burnett.

00:05:51.084 --> 00:05:52.751
-Yeah.
-She's just --

00:05:52.751 --> 00:05:55.387
I can't say enough
nice things about her,

00:05:55.387 --> 00:05:56.989
and she walks around
with these little cards.

00:05:56.989 --> 00:05:57.990
They're the cutest thing.

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:00.058
if you can find one on eBay,
get one.

00:06:00.058 --> 00:06:03.129
They're -- it's a little
caricature

00:06:03.129 --> 00:06:05.398
that was drawn of her,
I think by a famous artist

00:06:05.398 --> 00:06:08.300
that her husband made,
that says "Carol's Cosmo,"

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:11.137
and it has the recipe
for the particular way

00:06:11.137 --> 00:06:13.439
she likes her cosmo, 'cause when
she travels all the time

00:06:13.439 --> 00:06:15.841
her husband wanted her to have
the comfort of the drink

00:06:15.841 --> 00:06:17.509
that he usually makes
for her perfectly.

00:06:17.509 --> 00:06:19.112
And, uh --
-That's so sweet.

00:06:19.112 --> 00:06:20.412
-Yeah. She gives it to the
restaurant

00:06:20.412 --> 00:06:22.581
when she gets there,
and it's amazing.

00:06:22.581 --> 00:06:24.383
-'Cause she is -- the two things
I've heard about her,

00:06:24.383 --> 00:06:26.185
is she's like, one of the nicest
people in the world,

00:06:26.185 --> 00:06:29.322
but if you get her drink wrong,
she loses her [bleep].

00:06:29.322 --> 00:06:31.124
[ Laughter ]

00:06:31.124 --> 00:06:32.858
-No, exactly, no, she does.

00:06:32.858 --> 00:06:34.092
I think it's, you know, it's --

00:06:34.092 --> 00:06:35.828
-I think it also
must be so nice,

00:06:35.828 --> 00:06:38.897
because I think that --
and I -- this speaks

00:06:38.897 --> 00:06:42.635
to how intricately plotted
it is, and how --

00:06:42.635 --> 00:06:45.270
how much work has gone
into "Better Call Saul" --

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:47.840
no one could fake
that they watch it.

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:49.341
Does that make sense?
-Oh, true. Yeah.

00:06:49.341 --> 00:06:51.043
-Like, when she starts telling
you she likes the show,

00:06:51.043 --> 00:06:53.913
and asking about it, like,
it must be so rewarding to know,

00:06:53.913 --> 00:06:54.981
"Oh, this is definitely somebody

00:06:54.981 --> 00:06:56.014
who's paying attention
to the show."

00:06:56.014 --> 00:06:57.383
-She -- she does, yeah.
She does.

00:06:57.383 --> 00:07:00.353
And like, wait until you see
what she does in the show.

00:07:00.353 --> 00:07:04.156
Of course everybody knew that
she's tremendously talented

00:07:04.156 --> 00:07:07.626
and an amazing actress, but I --
I had never seen her do

00:07:07.626 --> 00:07:09.395
the breadth of work that you're
about to see.

00:07:09.395 --> 00:07:10.496
-That's very exciting.
-It's amazing.

00:07:10.496 --> 00:07:12.497
-That's a perfect
spoiler-free teaser.

00:07:12.497 --> 00:07:13.699
You're very good at this.

00:07:13.699 --> 00:07:15.200
-Thanks.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:16.702
-You, um --
-By the way, I have had somebody

00:07:16.702 --> 00:07:17.904
fake that they've seen the show.

00:07:17.904 --> 00:07:18.937
-Really?
-Yeah, drunk --

00:07:18.937 --> 00:07:20.139
drunk guy at a party.

00:07:20.139 --> 00:07:21.774
But, was like, going on and on
about how he much

00:07:21.774 --> 00:07:23.476
he loved it, and then he asked
me who I play.

00:07:23.476 --> 00:07:25.077
[ Laughter ]

00:07:25.077 --> 00:07:27.780
I was like, "The blonde woman."

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:30.482
-Oh, no.
That's really bad.

00:07:30.482 --> 00:07:32.084
Like, you didn't see --
have much of a plan at all.

00:07:32.084 --> 00:07:35.388
-No, no plan.
-You filmed in Albuquerque,

00:07:35.388 --> 00:07:39.124
and I did not realize
that you -- your roommates

00:07:39.124 --> 00:07:40.559
over the course
of the last few seasons

00:07:40.559 --> 00:07:43.562
were your co-stars Bob Odenkirk
and Patrick Fabian.

00:07:43.562 --> 00:07:45.364
-Yes.
-So how did that start,

00:07:45.364 --> 00:07:47.265
that you decided
all to live together?

00:07:47.265 --> 00:07:49.502
-Well, we kept having to --
we all lived apart

00:07:49.502 --> 00:07:51.270
and were in
different rentals there,

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:53.472
and constantly needed to
rehearse scenes,

00:07:53.472 --> 00:07:55.040
we'd rehearse them ad nauseam,

00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:57.677
and run the lines
and talk about the scenes.

00:07:57.677 --> 00:08:01.613
And it's very hard with people
doing 14 hour days

00:08:01.613 --> 00:08:03.282
and late nights trying
to meet up,

00:08:03.282 --> 00:08:05.484
and then also
it's just very lonely.

00:08:05.484 --> 00:08:08.053
And all of our spouses,
are friends, and partners

00:08:08.053 --> 00:08:09.888
are friends, and families
are friends.

00:08:09.888 --> 00:08:13.593
And eventually Bob said --
I think it was like

00:08:13.593 --> 00:08:15.728
season three, he said,
"Do you and Patrick

00:08:15.728 --> 00:08:17.163
want to get a house together?"

00:08:17.163 --> 00:08:20.633
And I said no for that season,
because it was one of those

00:08:20.633 --> 00:08:22.101
things where like when
you get out of college

00:08:22.101 --> 00:08:24.436
and a friend -- like we were
really close friends.

00:08:24.436 --> 00:08:25.437
-Yeah.
-You can ruin that

00:08:25.437 --> 00:08:26.572
really fast by being roommates.

00:08:26.572 --> 00:08:28.040
-Oh, absolutely. Yah.
-And I was like,

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:30.109
"Why ruin a good thing?"

00:08:30.109 --> 00:08:34.247
So they lived together down
the street, and Patrick Fabian

00:08:34.247 --> 00:08:35.914
put like rock posters
on their doors

00:08:35.914 --> 00:08:37.917
and made it into this
really bad college house.

00:08:37.917 --> 00:08:41.320
-[ Laughs ]
-But I just ended up spending

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:45.057
every -- every free -- all my
free time down there anyway,

00:08:45.057 --> 00:08:48.527
so then the following year
I said yes, and so for three --

00:08:48.527 --> 00:08:51.263
I guess three -- the last season
we lived together for a year,

00:08:51.263 --> 00:08:54.300
because it took a year
to shoot 13 episodes.

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:56.335
But yeah, we're very close.
-And your friendship survives.

00:08:56.335 --> 00:08:57.769
-It did.
Did you ever live with them?

00:08:57.769 --> 00:08:59.104
I mean, you've known
Bob for ever.

00:08:59.104 --> 00:09:00.139
-No, but I've never lived
with him.

00:09:00.139 --> 00:09:01.140
I've almost lived
with no TV stars.

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:02.308
-You should try it.
Yeah.

00:09:02.308 --> 00:09:04.210
No?
[ Laughter ]

00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:06.111
-I have so much more to ask.

00:09:06.111 --> 00:09:08.347
We'll be right back with more
from Rhea Seehorn, everybody.

